INT. HOTEL SERVICE ELEVATOR - DAY

A stainless box, too small. A three-tier wedding cake on a rolling cart takes up most of it. Six people squeeze around it.

CHEF BASILE ORTEGA, 50s, apron, sweat. SHANICE DORSEY, 20s, bridal planner, clipboard. UNCLE VITO PALAZZO, 60s, tuxedo, too tight. DEV RAMASWAMY, 20s, the photographer, camera bag. TILDA BJORKLUND, 30s, bridesmaid in a puffy teal gown. And the BRIDE herself, MOIRA KENNEDY, 28, in a veil, eyes closed, breathing slow.

The elevator jolts. Stops. The lights flicker.

TILDA
Oh no.

SHANICE
It's fine. It's a service elevator. They stall.

BASILE
Stall! Cake, madame, is made of stall!

VITO
Somebody push the button.

DEV
I'm pushing it.

SHANICE
Don't push it, it's the alarm.

TILDA
Everything is the alarm!

MOIRA
(eyes closed)
Everybody stop.

Nobody stops.

BASILE
Fondant! The humidity!

VITO
Is it hot in here?

DEV
It's very hot in here.

SHANICE
Please. Everyone please.

TILDA
My dress is sticking to my...

MOIRA
Everybody.

Dev leans on the wall. The cart rolls an inch. The cake sways. Everyone lunges at once. Basile catches the top tier. Vito catches Basile. Shanice catches Vito. Tilda catches Shanice's clipboard. The cake settles.

BASILE
Do not breathe!

VITO
I'm not breathing.

SHANICE
Can we call someone?

TILDA
I have no signal!

DEV
I have one bar.

BASILE
Zero bars on the cake!

MOIRA
(opening her eyes)
I'm leaving him.

Silence. The cake swings.

TILDA
What?

MOIRA
I'm leaving him. I've been thinking about it for three weeks, and I'm leaving him, and I'm doing it in the elevator.

SHANICE
Moira, honey.

VITO
Not on the cake.

The elevator lurches back to life. It rises. It dings. The doors open on a ballroom of two hundred guests, all of them turned to look.
